I compared testing.md against all 33 test files, CMakeLists.txt, check.hpp, architecture.md, headers.md and main.cpp. I’ve written an updated testing.md that applies the changes below.

I didn’t compile anything. The scenario count (about 230) is my own tally from reading the golden test’s source, and check.hpp’s location is unconfirmed (below). The golden file in tests/golden/ and the -pthread linking were also not checked.

Things in the current testing.md that are wrong or stale
The closing note is wrong. CMakeLists.txt now includes neighbor_fx in the plain unit-test loop (it’s the last name in the list). The note says it isn’t built, and the test list above it omits it. I removed the warning and added neighbor_fx to the list.
“About 180 renders.” Counting the scenarios in golden_render_test.cpp gives roughly 230. The histogram scenarios (about 48 fingerprints) were probably added after the 180 figure was written. The doc now says “roughly 230” and that the test prints the exact count each run.
“The unit tests share a harness.” Four files don’t use check.hpp: minijson_test, exif_test, detail_layer_test and neighbor_fx_test. Each defines its own CHECK and prints its own summary. They still return a non-zero exit code, so ctest works.
“A note about this documentation.” This said the tests weren’t read. I replaced it with a per-test coverage table.
Temp-folder tests. The doc mentioned only file_utils and image_meta. image_codec and thumb_worker use temp folders too.
Things missing that I added
Golden test details. Fresh checkouts fail with exit code 2 until you record the file. The doc covers --dump for diffing PPMs and the MISMATCH/NEW/MISSING/INVARIANT FAILED/note: output. It also notes that the sidecar round-trip is only a note: and does not fail the run.
Golden build gotchas.
With single-config generators, $<CONFIG> is empty unless you set CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE, so the file would be named render_.txt.
The golden target is built without LITEROOM_ONNX, so it never exercises the ONNX path.
GOLDEN_USE_ENGINE_HEADERS is not set in CMake yet. The doc lists which main.cpp names the test reaches into, so a refactor keeps them reachable.
Slider changes and golden fingerprints. The old “may gain new renders” line was incomplete. Adding a slider also changes adj/all_*, the sink/* scenarios, mask/every_effect, mask/composite_every_effect and sidecar/sink_json. Inserting one mid-table renumbers every later adj/NN_* name, so each shows as NEW plus MISSING.
Thread-test practices. Gates and latches with timeouts, CHECK only on the main thread, and a loop recommendation for thumb_worker as well as latest_worker.
A sanitizers section. Several tests ask to be run under sanitizers, but CMake has no switch for it.
A platform-differences section, plus an expanded “Where a new test belongs” list.
Problems the tests exposed (listed under “Known gaps” in the doc)
detail_layer_test and neighbor_fx_test start std::threads but are built in the plain loop without Threads::Threads. neighbor_fx_test’s own header says to build with -pthread. It links on modern toolchains but could fail on older Linux.
sam_segmenter.hpp has no test. kJpegMaxSide is declared but its enforcement is untested.
Questions for you
Does check.hpp live in tests/ or next to main.cpp? The file’s comment says “this folder”, but CMake’s include path is only the source root, so I left the location out of the doc.
histogram_test_1.cpp is byte-identical to histogram_test.cpp. If it’s in tests/, it’s a stray copy that CMake doesn’t build.